# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "A larger program".
# The source is a C function that returns res. It is written here in the language of the course,
# and the result of the function is the variable res. "x = 0", "x += 5" and "x -= 5" become assignments.
id: two-conditionals
title: Two conditionals in sequence
topic: Conditional
level: 3
source: Built from HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "A larger program"
tags: conditional, sequence, empty else, case analysis

task:
  The first conditional has no else branch: when $x < 0$ is false, it
  does nothing, like `skip`.

  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, from the last statement back. For each conditional, first
  calculate the weakest precondition of each branch, then the one of the
  conditional. Then use the consequence rule to decide whether the triple
  is valid.

given:
  Condition B1: $x < 0$
  Condition B2: $y < 0$

pre: -5 <= y <= 5 && -5 <= x <= 5
post: -15 <= res <= 25

program:
  {P}
  {R6}
  if x < 0 then {
    {R5}
    x := 0             // i1
    {R4}
  }
  {R4}
  if y < 0 then {
    {R2}
    x := x + 5         // i2
    {R1}
  } else {
    {R3}
    x := x - 5         // i3
    {R1}
  }
  {R1}
  res := x - y         // i4
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of res := x - y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i4
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($res$) with the assignment expression ($x - y$) in
  the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(res := x - y, Q)
  = (-15 <= res <= 25)[res <- x - y]
  = -15 <= x - y <= 25            // res is replaced by x - y
set: R1 = -15 <= x - y <= 25

step: R2, the weakest precondition of the then branch x := x + 5
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  Each branch of the second conditional ends before `res := x - y`, so
  its postcondition is $R1$. We substitute the assigned variable ($x$)
  with the assignment expression ($x + 5$) in $R1$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 5, R1)
  = (-15 <= x - y <= 25)[x <- x + 5]
  = -15 <= x + 5 - y <= 25        // x is replaced by x + 5
  = -20 <= x - y <= 20            // subtract 5 from the three parts
set: R2 = -20 <= x - y <= 20

step: R3, the weakest precondition of the else branch x := x - 5
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment
  expression ($x - 5$) in $R1$.
calc:
  wp(x := x - 5, R1)
  = (-15 <= x - y <= 25)[x <- x - 5]
  = -15 <= x - 5 - y <= 25        // x is replaced by x - 5
  = -10 <= x - y <= 30            // add 5 to the three parts
set: R3 = -10 <= x - y <= 30

step: R4, the weakest precondition of the second conditional
rule: IF, WP
focus: if y < 0, R2, R3
text:
  The weakest precondition of `if B then c1 else c2` for a postcondition
  $R$ is
  >> (B ==> wp(c1, R)) && (!B ==> wp(c2, R))
  Here $R$ is $R1$, the condition $B$ is $B2$, that is $y < 0$,
  $wp(c1, R1)$ is $R2$ and $wp(c2, R1)$ is $R3$:
  >>> («y < 0 : B2» ==> «R2») && («!(y < 0) : ¬B2» ==> «R3»)
calc:
  wp(if y < 0 then x := x + 5 else x := x - 5, R1)
  = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= x - y <= 20) && (!(y < 0) ==> -10 <= x - y <= 30)   // B, R2 and R3 put in the formula
  = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= x - y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= x - y <= 30)     // !(y < 0) is y >= 0
set: R4 = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= x - y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= x - y <= 30)

step: R5, the weakest precondition of the then branch x := 0
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  The first conditional ends where the second one starts, so the
  postcondition of its then branch is $R4$. We substitute the assigned
  variable ($x$) with the assignment expression ($0$) in $R4$.
calc:
  wp(x := 0, R4)
  = ((y < 0 ==> -20 <= x - y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= x - y <= 30))[x <- 0]
  = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= 0 - y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= 0 - y <= 30)   // every x is replaced by 0
  = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= -y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= -y <= 30)         // 0 - y is -y
  = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -30 <= y <= 10)           // multiply the three parts by -1, which reverses the order
  = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= y) && (y >= 0 ==> -30 <= y <= 10)                 // when y < 0, y <= 20 always holds
  = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= y) && (y >= 0 ==> y <= 10)                        // when y >= 0, -30 <= y always holds
set: R5 = (y < 0 ==> -20 <= y) && (y >= 0 ==> y <= 10)

step: R6, the weakest precondition of the first conditional
rule: IF, SKIP, WP
focus: if x < 0, R5, R4
text:
  The missing else branch does nothing, like `skip`, so its weakest
  precondition is its postcondition $R4$. The rule for the conditional,
  with the condition $B1$, that is $x < 0$, then gives
  >>> («x < 0 : B1» ==> «R5») && («!(x < 0) : ¬B1» ==> «R4»)
calc:
  wp(if x < 0 then x := 0, R4)
  = (x < 0 ==> (y < 0 ==> -20 <= y) && (y >= 0 ==> y <= 10)) && (!(x < 0) ==> (y < 0 ==> -20 <= x - y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= x - y <= 30))   // B, R5 and R4 put in the formula
  = (x < 0 ==> (y < 0 ==> -20 <= y) && (y >= 0 ==> y <= 10)) && (x >= 0 ==> (y < 0 ==> -20 <= x - y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= x - y <= 30))      // !(x < 0) is x >= 0
set: R6 = (x < 0 ==> (y < 0 ==> -20 <= y) && (y >= 0 ==> y <= 10)) && (x >= 0 ==> (y < 0 ==> -20 <= x - y <= 20) && (y >= 0 ==> -10 <= x - y <= 30))

step: P implies R6
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
focus: P, R6
text:
  $R6$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R6$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R6»
vc: -5 <= y <= 5 && -5 <= x <= 5 ==> R6
why:
  $R6$ has one case for each sign of $x$ and each sign of $y$. In each
  case we use the bounds $-5 <= x <= 5$ and $-5 <= y <= 5$ of $P$.

  - $x < 0$ and $y < 0$: we need $-20 <= y$. It holds since $-5 <= y$.
  - $x < 0$ and $y >= 0$: we need $y <= 10$. It holds since $y <= 5$.
  - $x >= 0$ and $y < 0$: we need $-20 <= x - y <= 20$. Here $0 <= x <= 5$ and $1 <= -y <= 5$, so $1 <= x - y <= 10$.
  - $x >= 0$ and $y >= 0$: we need $-10 <= x - y <= 30$. Here $0 <= x <= 5$ and $-5 <= -y <= 0$, so $-5 <= x - y <= 5$.

  All four cases hold, so $P$ implies $R6$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  A conditional splits the weakest precondition into two cases. With two
  conditionals in sequence the cases multiply, and the final check is a
  case analysis over the four combinations of the two conditions.
